From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8280xp: limit the video core clock to 444
 MHz

The iris driver counts the raw input frame in the VSP term of its clock
estimate for an encoder session, so even one real-time 1080p30 encode
asks for about 1.08 GHz and the video core clock (video_cc_mvs0_clk)
runs at the highest OPP, 560 MHz. At that clock the encoder's bitstream
engine (VSP) now and then stops in the middle of a frame. The firmware
watchdog then resets the hardware ("VCODEC HW is hung due to (CCE 0
VPP 0 VSP 1)"), three frames are dropped and coding restarts with an
IDR. On SC8280XP (Huawei MateBook E Go) this happened about once every
7,500 encoded frames, independent of the number of sessions and of CPU
idle states.

In one boot, with 300-frame 1080p H.264 encodes, 120,000 frames at the
driver's own choice of 560 MHz gave 13 hangs, while 60,000 frames each
with the clock pinned to 444 MHz and to 240 MHz through a test-only
driver parameter gave none. The MMCX and MX levels read back at
TURBO_L1 in all of these runs, and raising the DDR bandwidth vote to the
maximum at 560 MHz made no difference, so the hang follows the core
clock rather than the voltage or the memory bandwidth. The Windows
video driver for this SoC does not go above 444 MHz either: its highest
video P-state sets the MVS0 clock source to 1332 MHz (444 MHz after the
fixed divide-by-3) at MMCX NOM.

Drop the 533 and 560 MHz OPPs. 533 MHz was not measured; it goes as
well because the Windows driver never uses it. For larger requests
devfreq_recommended_opp() falls back to the highest remaining OPP, so
the core now runs at 444 MHz at most. At 444 MHz, single-stream
decoding of 3840x2160 HEVC (8-bit at 100 and 200 Mbit/s, 10-bit) and
VP9 paced at 120 fps still has no frame late by a frame period, unpaced
3840x2160 H.264 at 100 Mbit/s decodes at about 100 fps, and 3840x2160
encoding with prefilled input runs at about 160 fps, roughly 20% below
560 MHz.

With the two OPPs removed and no test parameter set, 120,000 frames of
the same encodes ran at 444 MHz without a hang, a dropped frame or an
extra IDR. No 300-frame round took more than 0.1 s longer than the
median, so the lower clock does not merely hide stalls that a longer
watchdog period would tolerate.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp.dtsi | 18 +++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp.dtsi
index b41857d250..258bfdfc34 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp.dtsi
@@ -4302,17 +4302,13 @@ opp-444000000 {
 							<&rpmhpd_opp_nom>;
 				};
 
-				opp-533000000 {
-					opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <533000000>;
-					required-opps = <&rpmhpd_opp_nom>,
-							<&rpmhpd_opp_turbo>;
-				};
-
-				opp-560000000 {
-					opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <560000000>;
-					required-opps = <&rpmhpd_opp_nom>,
-							<&rpmhpd_opp_turbo_l1>;
-				};
+				/*
+				 * No 533 and 560 MHz: at 560 MHz the encoder's VSP
+				 * occasionally stalls in mid-frame and the firmware
+				 * resets the hardware, which was not seen at 444 or
+				 * 240 MHz. The Windows driver does not go above
+				 * 444 MHz either.
+				 */
 			};
 		};
 
